# BUG-290 regression: Path.absolute() must ABSOLUTISE, not merely normalise.
#
# std.fs.path.resolve normalises ("a/../b" -> "b") but leaves ".", ".." and "foo"
# UNCHANGED and raises no error, so before the fix this was an identity function on
# exactly the input the name promises to handle.
#
# Both directions are asserted deliberately. The easy wrong fix -- prepend the cwd
# unconditionally -- passes the relative leg and corrupts the absolute one.
def main()
    var dot = "."

    # 1. A RELATIVE path must come back rooted.
    var abs = Path.absolute(dot)
    assert Path.isAbsolute(abs)
    assert not abs.startsWith(dot)
    assert abs.len > 1

    # 2. An ALREADY-ABSOLUTE path must survive. Idempotence is the check that
    #    catches a cwd being prepended to something already rooted.
    var again = Path.absolute(abs)
    assert Path.isAbsolute(again)
    assert again == abs

    # 3. It must agree with sys.cwd(), which is the other half of the same 0.16
    #    migration (realpathAlloc -> process.currentPathAlloc / fs.path.resolve).
    var cwd = sys.cwd()
    assert cwd.len > 0
    assert abs == cwd

    # NOT COVERED, and deliberately not faked: the error path. `catch _pp` used to
    # return the INPUT on failure, making a real error indistinguishable from
    # "already absolute". Exercising that needs allocation-failure injection, which
    # this harness has no way to do -- see BUG-291 for the same shape elsewhere.
    print("bug290: OK")
